R("9. **The cold reading of version 2.** [[V2READ]]",
  "9. **The cold reading of version 2.** On 11 October 2026 a new reader other than the writer (a separate Claude session, with the paper, the sources, the pieces, the logs and the compiled project, and the author's audit note of C53 only after its verdicts were written) read the changes from version 1 to version 2 of this certificate, the Lean of piece C53 and the changes of version 10 of the paper. It compared the fourteen statements of C53 with the piece symbol by symbol; re-elaborated the three files of C53 from source and ran `CheckC53.lean` and a check file of its own (the four `_all` statements and `T0`, with `#print axioms`); computed the small cases with Smith forms of its own (`n ≤ 5`); recounted the files, lines and declarations (420, 61 285, 3 470) and every column of Table 6b and §7; re-derived from `DepsCubo2.log` the union of the twenty and the 76 modules outside it; compared the Lean block of §1.5 with `CertCubo2.log` byte for byte; ran the acceptance list of the paper and its controls; and looked at every changed page. Verdict: the Lean of C53 is right, faithful and clean, and nothing mathematical changed. It found no error in a statement or a number, and nine points in this certificate and three in the paper: six sentences of the certificate that were false or inexact (the scope of this reading, «every statement of the paper», «each case is proved directly», the bold names of §3, the uniform run of §6 and the script of the tables) and three points of presentation (two sentences out of order, and `DW'_one`, `DW'_all` printed as formulas); in the paper, the scope of this reading in two sentences and the name «Theorem DW′». They are corrected here and in version 10 of the paper. What it did not check: Gao et al.'s range for their Theorem 4.1, Aristotle's register (541), the send time of C53, and the identity of the 68 earlier folders with the tarballs of the pieces that created them.", 'item 9')
